{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 1, "model_name": "mistralai/ministral-14b-instruct-2512", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli was a significant figure in the medieval history of the Levant particularly during the Crusades. Born in the 12th century he was the son of Raymond II Count of Tripoli and Hodierna of Jerusalem. His early years were marked by the political intrigues and military challenges that defined the Crusader states in the Holy Land.\n\n*highlighted section part 1*\n**Early Life and Succession**\nRaymond III inherited the County of Tripoli from his father Raymond II in 1187. The succession came at a critical time during the Third Crusade when Saladin was gaining prominence across the region. At the time of his inheritance Raymond was a child which meant the county was initially governed by a regent the most notable being his mother Hodierna. The regency period was turbulent with constant threats from Muslim forces and internal power struggles within the Crusader states.\n\nBy the time Raymond came of age around 1193 he faced substantial challenges. The County of Tripoli bordered Muslim territories including the powerful Ayyubid dynasty. The region was also marked by factionalism among Crusader nobles many of whom were more focused on their own ambitions than on unified resistance against external threats.\n\n*highlighted section part 2*\n**Military Campaigns and Diplomatic Efforts**\nRaymond III proved himself to be both a capable military leader and a skilled diplomat. He participated in several key Crusader campaigns during his reign. One of his notable early actions was his involvement in the siege of Sidon in 1197 where he played a crucial role in resisting Saladin’s forces. The siege lasted for several months but ultimately ended without a decisive victory for either side demonstrating the balance of power in the region.\n\nDiplomatically Raymond worked to maintain alliances with other Crusader states particularly with the Kingdom of Jerusalem and the Principality of Antioch. He also engaged in negotiations with Muslim rulers a strategy that allowed him to buy time and sometimes secure temporary truces. His diplomatic efforts included marrying Lucia of Montferrat widow of Conrad II of Montferrat who was also the brother of Isabella I of Jerusalem. This marriage not only strengthened his ties to the royal family of Jerusalem but also provided him with a powerful ally.\n\nRaymond III also took part in the Fourth Crusade though his role was more peripheral. The Crusaders’ diversion to Constantinople in 1204 rather than proceeding to the Holy Land had a significant impact on the Crusader states and Raymond was among those who had to adjust to the new geopolitical realities.\n\n*highlighted section part 3*\n**Later Years and Legacy**\nIn his later years Raymond III faced increasing pressure from the Ayyubid dynasty under the leadership of Al-Kamil who was the son of Saladin. The Muslim forces launched several offensives aimed at expanding their control over the Crusader states. By the 1220s Raymond found himself in a precarious position with his territories increasingly isolated and under threat.\n\nRaymond died in 1275 after a long reign that spanned almost six decades. His death marked the end of an era for the County of Tripoli. Under his leadership the county had managed to survive despite the overwhelming odds posed by the Muslim powers of the time. His reign saw a combination of military resistance diplomatic maneuvering and adaptation to the changing political landscape of the Levant.\n\nThe legacy of Raymond III is often overshadowed by more famous Crusader leaders such as Richard the Lionheart or Saladin. However his ability to maintain the independence of Tripoli for several decades speaks volumes about his skills as a ruler. The county he ruled remained one of the few Crusader states to retain a degree of autonomy and stability throughout the turbulent period of the Crusades. His efforts helped preserve a Christian presence in the Levant for generations to come.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Ray", "logprob": -0.08366527408361435}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": -0.0001662831346038729}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -0.005333361681550741}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -0.24919773638248444}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -0.00035089056473225355}, {"token": " Tripoli", "logprob": -0.01817992888391018}, {"token": " was", "logprob": -0.4510277509689331}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.09894715994596481}, {"token": " significant", "logprob": -2.2088003158569336}, {"token": " figure", "logprob": -1.0645983219146729}, {"token": " in", "logprob": -0.1555301398038864}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -0.20345687866210938}, {"token": " medieval", "logprob": -0.40165141224861145}, {"token": " history", "logprob": -1.7214442491531372}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -0.013515869155526161}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -0.017016949132084846}, {"token": " Levant", "logprob": -1.313670039176941}, {"token": " particularly", "logprob": -2.1903576850891113}, {"token": " during", "logprob": -0.4409678876399994}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -0.0025373664684593678}, {"token": " Crus", "logprob": -0.2652650475502014}, {"token": "ades", "logprob": -0.6969553232192993}, {"token": ".", "logprob": -0.3539575934410095}, {"token": " Born", "logprob": -0.3941638767719269}, {"token": " in", "logprob": -1.6529054641723633}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -0.2977181077003479}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -2.139735698699951}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -0.004760360810905695}, {"token": "2", "logprob": -0.2711678445339203}, {"token": "th", "logprob": -0.00405976502224803}, {"token": " century", "logprob": -0.0006258912617340684}, {"token": " he", "logprob": -0.5077774524688721}, {"token": " was", "logprob": -0.5788633227348328}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -0.08605971187353134}, {"token": " son", "logprob": -0.5702297687530518}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -0.0029847142286598682}, {"to